or less.

103
00:04:38,960 --> 00:04:40,960
So the story goes like this:

104
00:04:40,960 --> 00:04:41,960
Wyn Davis is an author

105
00:04:41,960 --> 00:04:43,960
and he wrote a book called Men of Shiff

106
00:04:43,960 --> 00:04:45,960
and we've talked about it on the blog

107
00:04:45,960 --> 00:04:47,960
and on the podcast before

108
00:04:47,960 --> 00:04:50,960
And once again I'll make sure that there's a link

109
00:04:50,960 --> 00:04:51,960
so that you can find that book.

110
00:04:51,960 --> 00:04:53,960
Go to scoutmastercgcom.

111
00:04:53,960 --> 00:04:55,960
Look at the post that contains this podcast,

112
00:04:55,960 --> 00:04:56,960
Podcast 178..

113
00:04:56,960 --> 00:04:58,960
You're going to find links to all kinds of stuff.

114
00:04:58,960 --> 00:05:01,960
I was looking at the website that Wyn set up for the book

115
00:05:01,960 --> 00:05:04,960
and he had on there this really interesting

116
00:05:04,960 --> 00:05:06,960
little artifact of scouting history:

117
00:05:06,960 --> 00:05:11,960
A video of Green Bar Bill Hillcourt-

118
00:05:11,960 --> 00:05:13,960
Green Bar Bill William Hillcourt.

119
00:05:13,960 --> 00:05:16,960
He authored many editions of the Scout Handbook

120
00:05:16,960 --> 00:05:18,960
and the Patrol Leaders Handbook

121
00:05:18,960 --> 00:05:20,960
and the original field book-

122
00:05:20,960 --> 00:05:23,960
A central figure in scouting for many, many years.

123
00:05:23,960 --> 00:05:25,960
He devoted his entire life to it.

124
00:05:25,960 --> 00:05:28,960
I was really thrilled to find this clip,

125
00:05:28,960 --> 00:05:30,960
which was from the early 80s,

126
00:05:30,960 --> 00:05:33,960
and it was just a video that somebody shot

127
00:05:33,960 --> 00:05:35,960
of a visit that Bill had paid to Florida.

128
00:05:35,960 --> 00:05:38,960
He was getting ready to go talk to a wood badge session,

129
00:05:38,960 --> 00:05:40,960
Invited some scouts and some leaders to come.

130
00:05:40,960 --> 00:05:42,960
It's just a discussion they were having,

131
00:05:42,960 --> 00:05:43,960
But in that discussion

132
00:05:43,960 --> 00:05:45,960
Bill says some very interesting things,

133
00:05:45,960 --> 00:05:47,960
and I think the one that I centered on

134
00:05:47,960 --> 00:05:51,960
is: what exactly is the promise of scouting?

135
00:05:51,960 --> 00:05:52,960
What are we doing?

136
00:05:52,960 --> 00:05:55,960
You know, if anybody is going to be an expert on that,

137
00:05:55,960 --> 00:05:57,960
it's going to be Bill Hillcourt.

138
00:05:57,960 --> 00:06:00,960
He's the guy who literally wrote the book right,

139
00:06:00,960 --> 00:06:01,960
And when we're talking about him,

140
00:06:01,960 --> 00:06:03,960
we're talking about the entire history of scouting,

141
00:06:03,960 --> 00:06:06,960
all the way back to Baden Powell.

142
00:06:06,960 --> 00:06:10,960
He was quite a bit younger than Baden Powell,

143
00:06:10,960 --> 00:06:12,960
but he knew Baden Powell very well

144
00:06:12,960 --> 00:06:14,960
and he actually wrote a biography of Baden Powell.

145
00:06:14,960 --> 00:06:17,960
I think if we're going to look for an authoritative source

146
00:06:17,960 --> 00:06:18,960
as to what scouting ought to be,

147
00:06:18,960 --> 00:06:20,960
I think Bill is going to be the man.

148
00:06:20,960 --> 00:06:22,960
I'm going to play for you something that Bill said:

149
00:06:22,960 --> 00:06:24,960
Scoutmasters should be trained

150
00:06:24,960 --> 00:06:28,960
to make every single promise on page 9..

151
00:06:28,960 --> 00:06:31,960
Come true in the lives of your kids.

152
00:06:31,960 --> 00:06:32,960
So he said there,

153
00:06:32,960 --> 00:06:34,960
Scoutmasters should be trained

154
00:06:34,960 --> 00:06:37,960
to make every single promise on page 9..

155
00:06:37,960 --> 00:06:41,960
Come true in the life of your kids, right?

156
00:06:41,960 --> 00:06:42,960
That's what he said.

157
00:06:42,960 --> 00:06:44,960
Well, what's page 9 is he referring to?

158
00:06:44,960 --> 00:06:46,960
Here's a little bit more history.

159
00:06:46,960 --> 00:06:47,960
In the late 70s

160
00:06:47,960 --> 00:06:49,960
Bill Hillcourt came out of retirement

161
00:06:49,960 --> 00:06:53,960
and volunteered his time to rewrite the Scout Handbook.

162
00:06:53,960 --> 00:06:54,960
In the early 70s

163
00:06:54,960 --> 00:06:56,960
the BSA had initiated some program changes

164
00:06:56,960 --> 00:06:58,960
and things that didn't work really well.

165
00:06:58,960 --> 00:06:59,960
Bill didn't like them.

166
00:06:59,960 --> 00:07:00,960
He said:

167
00:07:00,960 --> 00:07:02,960
I want to write the Scout Handbook again.

168
00:07:02,960 --> 00:07:03,960
I want to get you guys back on track.

169
00:07:03,960 --> 00:07:05,960
He came out of retirement,

170
00:07:05,960 --> 00:07:07,960
spent a year or two writing the Handbook.

171
00:07:07,960 --> 00:07:08,960
What he's talking about here

172
00:07:08,960 --> 00:07:11,960
is page 9 of that edition of the Scout Handbook.

173
00:07:11,960 --> 00:07:14,960
That is the 9th edition that was first published in 1979..

174
00:07:14,960 --> 00:07:16,960
Let me read that page to you, okay,

175
00:07:16,960 --> 00:07:17,960
And it's titled

176
00:07:17,960 --> 00:07:18,960
Your Life as a Scout.

177
00:07:18,960 --> 00:07:20,960
You are an American boy

178
00:07:20,960 --> 00:07:21,960
and before long

179
00:07:21,960 --> 00:07:22,960
you're going to be an American man.

180
00:07:22,960 --> 00:07:24,960
It's important to America

181
00:07:24,960 --> 00:07:26,960
and to yourself

182
00:07:26,960 --> 00:07:29,960
that you become a citizen of fine character,

183
00:07:29,960 --> 00:07:30,960
physically strong,

184
00:07:30,960 --> 00:07:31,960
mentally awake

185
00:07:31,960 --> 00:07:32,960
and morally straight.

186
00:07:32,960 --> 00:07:34,960
So I think we can all agree with that.

187
00:07:34,960 --> 00:07:36,960
That's a really great goal to have.

188
00:07:36,960 --> 00:07:37,960
Bill went on to say this:

189
00:07:37,960 --> 00:07:38,960
He said,

190
00:07:38,960 --> 00:07:40,960
Boy Scouting will help you become

191
00:07:40,960 --> 00:07:41,960
that kind of citizen,

192
00:07:41,960 --> 00:07:42,960
but also

193
00:07:42,960 --> 00:07:45,960
Scouting will give you fellowship and fun.

194
00:07:45,960 --> 00:07:47,960
The fellowship and the fun, folks,

195
00:07:47,960 --> 00:07:49,960
is the way that you become that kind of citizen.

196
00:07:49,960 --> 00:07:51,960
So how does that happen?

197
00:07:51,960 --> 00:07:52,960
Bill goes on to explain it.

198
00:07:52,960 --> 00:07:53,960
He says this:

199
00:07:53,960 --> 00:07:54,960
Yes, it's fun to be a Boy Scout.

200
00:07:54,960 --> 00:07:56,960
It's fun to go hiking and camping

201
00:07:56,960 --> 00:07:57,960
with all your best friends,

202
00:07:57,960 --> 00:07:58,960
to swim, to dive,

203
00:07:58,960 --> 00:07:59,960
to paddle a canoe,

204
00:07:59,960 --> 00:08:00,960
to wield an axe,

205
00:08:00,960 --> 00:08:02,960
to follow the footsteps of the pioneers

206
00:08:02,960 --> 00:08:04,960
who led the way through the wilderness,

207
00:08:04,960 --> 00:08:06,960
to stare into the glowing embers of a campfire

208
00:08:06,960 --> 00:08:08,960
and to dream of the wonders of the life

209
00:08:08,960 --> 00:08:10,960
that is in store for you.

210
00:08:10,960 --> 00:08:12,960
It's also fun to learn to walk

211
00:08:12,960 --> 00:08:14,960
noiselessly through the woods,

212
00:08:14,960 --> 00:08:16,960
to stalk close to grazing deer

213
00:08:16,960 --> 00:08:17,960
without being noticed,

214
00:08:17,960 --> 00:08:18,960
to bring a bird close to you

215
00:08:18,960 --> 00:08:20,960
by imitating its call.

216
00:08:20,960 --> 00:08:21,960
It's fun to find your way

217
00:08:21,960 --> 00:08:23,960
cross-country by map and compass,

218
00:08:23,960 --> 00:08:25,960
to make a meal when you're hungry,

219
00:08:25,960 --> 00:08:27,960
to take a safe swim when you are not,

220
00:08:27,960 --> 00:08:29,960
to make yourself comfortable for the night

221
00:08:29,960 --> 00:08:31,960
and attend under the stars.

222
00:08:31,960 --> 00:08:32,960
In Scouting

223
00:08:32,960 --> 00:08:34,960
you become an outdoorsman.

224
00:08:34,960 --> 00:08:36,960
But Scouting is far more than fun

225
00:08:36,960 --> 00:08:37,960
in the outdoors, hiking and camping,

226
00:08:37,960 --> 00:08:39,960
Scouting is a way of life.

227
00:08:39,960 --> 00:08:42,960
Scouting is growing into responsible manhood,

228
00:08:42,960 --> 00:08:46,960
learning to be of service to others.

229
00:08:46,960 --> 00:08:48,960
The Scout Oath and the Scout Law

230
00:08:48,960 --> 00:08:50,960
are your guides to citizenship.

231
00:08:50,960 --> 00:08:53,960
They tell you what is expected of a scout.

232
00:08:53,960 --> 00:08:55,960
They point out your duties.

233
00:08:55,960 --> 00:08:57,960
The Scout motto is: be prepared.

234
00:08:57,960 --> 00:08:59,960
Prepare to take care of yourself

235
00:08:59,960 --> 00:09:01,960
and to help other people in need.

236
00:09:01,960 --> 00:09:04,960
The Scout slogan is: do a good turn daily

237
00:09:04,960 --> 00:09:07,960
Together. the motto and the slogan

238
00:09:07,960 --> 00:09:09,960
spell out your ability

239
00:09:09,960 --> 00:09:11,960
and your willingness to serve.

240
00:09:11,960 --> 00:09:13,960
Your life as a Scout will make you strong

241
00:09:13,960 --> 00:09:15,960
and self-reliant.

242
00:09:15,960 --> 00:09:19,960
You will learn Scoutcraft skills

243
00:09:19,960 --> 00:09:21,960
that will benefit you as you grow.

244
00:09:21,960 --> 00:09:23,960
In time you will develop the skills

245
00:09:23,960 --> 00:09:25,960
of leadership as well.

246
00:09:25,960 --> 00:09:27,960
So pitch in, swing into action

247
00:09:27,960 --> 00:09:29,960
In your patrol and in your troop.

248
00:09:29,960 --> 00:09:31,960
you will have some of the best times

249
00:09:31,960 --> 00:09:32,960
of your life.

250
00:09:32,960 --> 00:09:35,960
That is what Bill wrote on page 9..

251
00:09:35,960 --> 00:09:37,960
I'll remind you again what he said:

252
00:09:37,960 --> 00:09:39,960
Scout masters should be trained

253
00:09:39,960 --> 00:09:42,960
to make every single promise on page 9

254
00:09:42,960 --> 00:09:44,960
come true in the life of Scouts.

255
00:09:44,960 --> 00:09:47,960
What he was doing, at least in my way of thinking,

256
00:09:47,960 --> 00:09:49,960
was reaffirming that Scouting was not something

257
00:09:49,960 --> 00:09:52,960
to approach formulaically through a system of academics,

258
00:09:52,960 --> 00:09:55,960
but that it was a progressive developmental journey

259
00:09:55,960 --> 00:09:58,960
through experiences in the outdoors.

260
00:09:58,960 --> 00:10:01,960
On page 9, he lays out the sum total

261
00:10:01,960 --> 00:10:04,960
of what he had learned through some 60 years of Scouting

262
00:10:04,960 --> 00:10:07,960
and his definition of what Scouting should be

263
00:10:07,960 --> 00:10:10,960
and what every Scouter should be aimed at creating.

264
00:10:10,960 --> 00:10:13,960
What he doesn't talk about on page 9

265
00:10:13,960 --> 00:10:16,960
is almost as important as what he does mention.

266
00:10:16,960 --> 00:10:19,960
Nowhere on page 9 does he mention being an Eagle Scouter,

267
00:10:19,960 --> 00:10:21,960
even suggest the subject of advancement.

268
00:10:21,960 --> 00:10:25,960
Only parenthetically does he refer to leadership skills.

269
00:10:25,960 --> 00:10:27,960
Of course, neither advancement to Eagle

270
00:10:27,960 --> 00:10:29,960
nor developing leadership skills

271
00:10:29,960 --> 00:10:31,960
are bad things in themselves-

272
00:10:31,960 --> 00:10:32,960
Certainly not.

273
00:10:32,960 --> 00:10:35,960
But they have become wildly over-emphasized

274
00:10:35,960 --> 00:10:37,960
and are preoccupation with them

275
00:10:37,960 --> 00:10:41,960
shoulders out the other essential things about Scouting.

276
00:10:41,960 --> 00:10:43,960
Is the most important thing about Scouting

277
00:10:43,960 --> 00:10:45,960
the outdoor program?

278
00:10:45,960 --> 00:10:46,960
Yes,

279
00:10:46,960 --> 00:10:47,960
Yes, it is,

280
00:10:47,960 --> 00:10:50,960
But not because the skills of the outdoors

281
00:10:50,960 --> 00:10:52,960
are particularly important,

282
00:10:52,960 --> 00:10:56,960
but because the process of learning these skills

283
00:10:56,960 --> 00:10:59,960
and the process of preparing for

284
00:10:59,960 --> 00:11:01,960
and participating in outdoor experiences

285
00:11:01,960 --> 00:11:04,960
as a member of your patrol are important.

286
00:11:04,960 --> 00:11:07,960
They are in fact so important

287
00:11:07,960 --> 00:11:11,960
that Hillcourt says making those processes

288
00:11:11,960 --> 00:11:14,960
a reality is the only thing

289
00:11:14,960 --> 00:11:16,960
a Scoutmaster should be concerned with.

290
00:11:16,960 --> 00:11:19,960
When Scouts do the things that Scouts do,

291
00:11:19,960 --> 00:11:21,960
when they go camping, when they get outdoors,

292
00:11:21,960 --> 00:11:24,960
when they do all the wonderful things

293
00:11:24,960 --> 00:11:26,960
that Bill pictured here on page 9,

294
00:11:26,960 --> 00:11:27,960
they advance.

295
00:11:27,960 --> 00:11:28,960
They can't help it.

296
00:11:28,960 --> 00:11:29,960
They have to advance

297
00:11:29,960 --> 00:11:32,960
because they're doing the things that Scouts do.

298
00:11:32,960 --> 00:11:33,960
Let's remember this:

299
00:11:33,960 --> 00:11:36,960
When Scouts do the things Scouts do,

300
00:11:36,960 --> 00:11:37,960
they advance.

301
00:11:37,960 --> 00:11:40,960
When Scouts do the things Scouts do,

302
00:11:40,960 --> 00:11:42,960
they learn how to lead.

303
00:11:42,960 --> 00:11:44,960
They learn how to lead

304
00:11:44,960 --> 00:11:47,960
because Scouts do things in a patrol,

305
00:11:47,960 --> 00:11:49,960
And to have a patrol go out and do things,

306
00:11:49,960 --> 00:11:52,960
you can't have an adult take care of the patrol.

307
00:11:52,960 --> 00:11:53,960
It has to be a scout,

308
00:11:53,960 --> 00:11:55,960
It has to be a patrol leader.

309
00:11:55,960 --> 00:11:58,960
And when you put a bunch of patrols together in a troop,

310
00:11:58,960 --> 00:12:00,960
you can't have an adult take care of the troop.

311
00:12:00,960 --> 00:12:02,960
You have to have a senior patrol leader

312
00:12:02,960 --> 00:12:04,960
who has to, along with the patrol leaders,

313
00:12:04,960 --> 00:12:08,960
develop and plan and present a program of activities.

314
00:12:08,960 --> 00:12:10,960
So when Scouts do that-

315
00:12:10,960 --> 00:12:11,960
that's what Scouts do-

316
00:12:11,960 --> 00:12:14,960
they learn to lead.

317
00:12:14,960 --> 00:12:16,960
And when Scouts do what Scouts do,

318
00:12:16,960 --> 00:12:19,960
they achieve the aims of scouting.

319
00:12:19,960 --> 00:12:21,960
And our aim is, just like Bill said,

320
00:12:21,960 --> 00:12:24,960
to become a citizen of fine character,

321
00:12:24,960 --> 00:12:27,960
physically strong, mentally awake and morally straight.

322
00:12:27,960 --> 00:12:28,960
So let's think about this:

323
00:12:28,960 --> 00:12:31,960
When Scouts do the things that Scouts do,

324
00:12:31,960 --> 00:12:34,960
they advance, they learn how to lead.

325
00:12:34,960 --> 00:12:37,960
and all of that, that big process,

326
00:12:37,960 --> 00:12:39,960
achieves the aims of scouting.

327
00:12:39,960 --> 00:12:42,960
Now, when Scouts do requirements

328
00:12:42,960 --> 00:12:44,960
and when they go to merit badge classes

329
00:12:44,960 --> 00:12:47,960
and when they go to leadership training,

330
00:12:47,960 --> 00:12:50,960
they get badges and certificates.

331
00:12:50,960 --> 00:12:52,960
Not bad,

332
00:12:52,960 --> 00:12:54,960
Everybody likes a badge.

333
00:12:54,960 --> 00:12:57,960
I've got a whole wall full of certificates somewhere. right,

334
00:12:57,960 --> 00:12:59,960
But that's not the aim, is it?

335
00:12:59,960 --> 00:13:02,960
When Scouts do the things that Scouts do-

336
00:13:02,960 --> 00:13:05,960
and they do all that great stuff on page nine,

337
00:13:05,960 --> 00:13:08,960
they'll get the badges and certificates anyway.

338
00:13:08,960 --> 00:13:11,960
It's a matter of focus and aim.

339
00:13:11,960 --> 00:13:13,960
Are you getting your Scouts outdoors?

340
00:13:13,960 --> 00:13:15,960
Are they getting outdoors all the time?

341
00:13:15,960 --> 00:13:17,960
Is that what everybody's driven around?

342
00:13:17,960 --> 00:13:19,960
Are they doing the things that Scouts do

343
00:13:19,960 --> 00:13:20,960
in the Scout way?

344
00:13:20,960 --> 00:13:22,960
Are they planning and presenting

345
00:13:22,960 --> 00:13:25,960
and leading all of their activities?

346
00:13:25,960 --> 00:13:27,960
They can't help but advance.

347
00:13:27,960 --> 00:13:29,960
They can't help but learn to lead

348
00:13:29,960 --> 00:13:31,960
and they can't help but to achieve the aims of scouting.

349
00:13:31,960 --> 00:13:34,960
Greenbar Bill, good old Bill Hillcourt,

350
00:13:34,960 --> 00:13:37,960
dedicated his entire life to reminding us

351
00:13:37,960 --> 00:13:40,960
to get the horse in front of the cart.

352
00:13:40,960 --> 00:13:42,960
If you watch the entire video that I'm talking about,

353
00:13:42,960 --> 00:13:44,960
you get some idea of the clarity

354
00:13:44,960 --> 00:13:47,960
and intensity of this guy in his 80.

355
00:13:47,960 --> 00:13:50,960
Again and again he returns to the way scouting works.

356
00:13:50,960 --> 00:13:53,960
He cajoles and questions and encouraged the Scouts

357
00:13:53,960 --> 00:13:57,960
and adults in the room that night to get it right.

